export const lpContent={
'apple':{
 title:'Apple',one:'The first fruit most children name — shiny, heavy, cold, and probably within reach of your kitchen right now.',
 seoTitle:'Apple Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Fruit Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach apple with where it grows, a wash-and-crunch tasting routine and a fruit-bowl counting game for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The apple opens the fruit class because it is the fruit most children already own: it sits in their fruit bowl, their lunchbox, their stories. This lesson gives the word a home, the fruit a biography — it grows on trees — and your child a crunchy, washed-and-ready tasting job.',
 what:'An apple is a round fruit that grows on trees, in orchards and gardens, hanging from branches until picked. On the card it is a shiny red apple with a green leaf. In your kitchen it is heavy and cold in the hand, crunchy between the teeth, and sweet enough to request by name.',
 mean:'Naming a food your child already eats turns snack time into lesson time: the word apple gets attached to the heaviest, coldest, crunchiest fruit in the bowl, and every future apple repeats it. Food words are the easiest vocabulary a kitchen teaches, because the props show up daily and get eaten on schedule.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, one real apple held in both hands — heavy, smooth, cold — and one supervised tasting: washed under the tap, cut by a grown-up into slices, then crunched loudly together. The loud crunch is not optional; it is the sound of the lesson working.',
 why:'Apples are a first lesson in where food comes from: not shelves, but trees, seasons and picking. A child who says “apples grow on trees” has planted the seed of a food story that will grow into gardens, farms and why-we-wash-things — real knowledge with a snack attached.',
 dev:'Word-object mapping with a holdable prop, early food-science (tree to table), sensory vocabulary (heavy, smooth, cold, crunch), counting at the fruit bowl, and the confidence of requesting food by name.',
 vocab:'apple, red, round, crunch, tree, seed',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say apple — then ask your child to find the real thing in the fruit bowl before you lift a finger.\n2) Hand the apple over: heavy, smooth, cold. Say the word while both hands are full — the grip is the vocabulary lesson.\n3) Tell the biography: apples grow on trees, hung there by the tree itself, picked when round and ready.\n4) Tasting job: your child washes the apple under the tap while you cut it into slices, then everyone crunches as loudly as possible.\n5) Count the apples left in the bowl together — the word got said, the fruit got eaten, and the numbers sneaked in behind both.',
 say:'“Apple! Here is the picture apple, all shiny with its leaf. Can you find the real one? Feel it — heavy! Cold! Now we wash it, a grown-up cuts it, and... CRUNCH! Did you hear that? That is the sound of an apple saying hello.”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a shiny red apple with a green leaf and the word apple underneath. Ask your child where the leaf sits, what color the apple is, and whether the real apple in your bowl wears a leaf too.',
 ask:'“Where do apples grow — on trees or under the ground?” “Is the apple heavy or light?” “What sound does a crunch make?” “Should we wash it first — why?”',
 childSay:'Your child might demand “apple slices, please!” at odd hours, report that the apple is cold like the fridge, or march to the tap uninvited to wash the next one — a food word that triggers its own safety routine is a food word fully learned.',
 real:'Apples repeat all week: at breakfast, in the lunchbox, at the shop where the pile sits shiny and cold. Each one is a free rehearsal of the word — and by the time the bag is empty, it has been crunched into memory.',
 play:'Apple bowl hide-and-seek: hide three apples around the room while your child counts to ten, then send them hunting with the word — “find an apple!” Every discovery gets announced, washed and shared. The hunt is cardio; the naming is the lesson.',
 safety:'Apples get washed before every tasting, and a grown-up does all the cutting — slices, not chunks, for small mouths. Whole apples are for confident chewers at the table, never for wandering-and-munching, and a wobbly-toothed cruncher gets their slices thinner.',
 check:'Ask: “Where does an apple grow? What do we do before we eat it? Show me a crunch.” Tree, wash and a jaw-breaking demonstration — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child rejects the first slice, offer the crunch challenge instead: who can make the loudest sound? Pride reopens more mouths than persuasion ever will, and the second bite is usually the one that counts.'},
'banana':{
 title:'Banana',one:'A yellow crescent children can open themselves — one of the first foods a small hand ever unlocks without help.',
 seoTitle:'Banana Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Peel-It-Yourself Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach banana with where it grows, a peel-it-like-a-pro routine and a spotty-ripeness game for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The banana is the fruit a three-year-old can open all by themselves, which makes it a milestone wearing a snack. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the growing — tall plants in warm countries, not trees — and hands over the peel, the proudest job in the fruit bowl.',
 what:'A banana is a long, curved, yellow fruit that grows in bunches on tall plants in warm countries. The card shows one yellow banana with the word underneath. Inside the peel it is soft and sweet; outside it turns from green to yellow to spotty as it ripens — spots mean sweeter.',
 mean:'Peeling is problem-solving: pinch the top, pull the strip down, repeat. A child who works a banana open is doing fine-motor engineering with an edible result, and the word banana rides on every step — said at the pinch, said at the pull, said with a full mouth at the end.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the where-it-grows story — big plants, warm places, bunches — and one full peel done by their own hands while you supervise only the eating. The ripeness game is a bonus: green means wait, yellow means go, spots mean sweetest of all.',
 why:'Self-service foods build independence at exactly the age it is cheapest to build: a banana needs no knife, no grown-up strength and no waiting. The word attaches to capability — “I can open it myself” — and capability is the hook that keeps vocabulary coming back.',
 dev:'Fine motor control in the peel, sequencing (pinch, pull, open, eat), ripeness observation, tropical-plant knowledge (big plants, not trees), and the independence that makes snack time self-managing.',
 vocab:'banana, yellow, peel, soft, sweet, curve',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say banana slowly — three parts, three claps: ba-na-na. Clap it again together.\n2) Ask where it grows and tell the story: tall green plants in warm countries, bananas hanging in whole bunches like crowds of friends.\n3) Hand over the fruit and the job: your child pinches the top and peels the strip down — help only if the pinch fails twice.\n4) Say the word at every stage: banana in the hand, banana in the peel, banana in the smile.\n5) Finish with the spots game: find the spottiest banana in the bowl and guess whether it is the sweetest — then taste the evidence.',
 say:'“Banana! Ba-na-na — three claps for three parts. Where does it grow? On tall, tall plants, hanging in bunches in the sunshine. Now the best bit: you peel it. Pinch the top... pull the strip... open! Yellow all the way through. You opened it yourself!”',
 notice:'Study the card together: it shows a yellow banana, bent like a smile, with the word banana underneath. Ask your child which way the curve bends, what color it is, and whether the real bananas at home bend the same way.',
 ask:'“Do bananas grow on trees or on big plants?” “What color is a banana before it is ready?” “What do the spots mean?” “Who can peel one — you or me?”',
 childSay:'Your child might clap out ba-na-na at random meals, inspect every banana in the shop for spots, or peel one with exaggerated ceremony for a visiting teddy — the word has landed when the routine travels to an audience.',
 real:'Bananas arrive weekly and get opened daily: breakfast, snacks, the shopping bag. Each peel is another rehearsal of the word and another rep for the fingers that do it — vocabulary and fine motor, sharing one snack.',
 play:'Peel-it-like-a-pro practice: two bananas, and your child opens both — pinch the top, pull the strip down, one strip at a time — while you supervise the eating. Save the peels for a teddy-bear picnic afterwards, where the word gets performed to an audience of one very polite bear.',
 safety:'A grown-up takes over if the peel fights back, and the fruit gets a quick rinse before peeling — hands touch the outside, then the inside. Watch for over-enthusiastic bites: a banana is soft, but a full mouth still needs sitting down and slowing up.',
 check:'Ask: “What grows on tall plants in warm places? Clap the word. Who peels it?” The claps, the plants and a proudly opened fruit — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child calls every yellow food banana, spread the evidence: a lemon, a pepper, the banana itself — same color, different shapes. Sorting by shape after sorting by color is the next chapter, and the fruit bowl teaches it for free.'},
'orange':{
 title:'Orange',one:'The only food that shares its name with a color — and the smelliest handshake in the fruit bowl: the perfume starts before the peel is off.',
 seoTitle:'Orange Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Smell-First Fruit Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach orange with where it grows, a sniff-then-peel routine and a segment-counting snack for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The orange is the word-learning cheat: its name and its color are the same word, so every time your child says the fruit they are also practising the color. This lesson adds the smell — the peel’s perfume arrives before anything is even open — and the segments, which count beautifully.',
 what:'An orange is a round citrus fruit that grows on trees in sunny places, wearing a dimpled peel. The card shows a round orange with a green leaf. Under the peel sit segments in their own thin coats, and the smell — pressed out by little hands rolling it — starts before the first strip comes off.',
 mean:'One word with two meanings doubles its airtime: orange the fruit at snack, orange the color at craft. A child who holds both meanings has met homonyms — words that wear two hats — years before the word homonym exists, and the fruit bowl demonstrated it with smell included.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the roll-and-sniff trick — rolling the fruit on the table presses oil into the peel and wakes the smell — and one shared peel-and-count: a grown-up starts the peel, small hands pull strips, and the segments get counted before they vanish.',
 why:'Smell is the most underrated sense in vocabulary work: scent memories are strong and sticky, and a word that arrives with perfume attached gets a second anchor in the brain. Roll, sniff, peel, count — four senses doing the work of one word.',
 dev:'Olfactory vocabulary (smell, sniff, scent), fine motor in the peeling, counting the segments, two-meaning word awareness (fruit and color), and the table science of why rolling wakes a smell.',
 vocab:'orange, peel, smell, round, segments, juicy',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say orange — then hold up a real one and ask what else the word can mean. Let your child discover the double meaning.\n2) The smell trick: roll the orange firmly on the table, then sniff your palms together — the perfume lives in the peel, and rolling wakes it.\n3) A grown-up scores the peel; your child pulls the strips off, one by one, saying the word at every piece.\n4) Split the segments out and count them together before eating — then allow the loud, juicy munching.\n5) Close with the color round-up: what else in the kitchen is orange? The carrot card is coming, and it will match.',
 say:'“Orange! The fruit with two jobs — it is a fruit AND a color. Roll it, roll it... now sniff your hands! Can you smell the sunshine? Now we peel: strip by strip. And inside — look — little boats! Let us count the boats before we eat them.”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a round orange with a green leaf and the word orange underneath. Ask your child whether the fruit on the card is whole, where the leaf attaches, and what the real orange’s skin feels like next to the card’s smooth picture.',
 ask:'“Can one word mean two things?” “What color is an orange?” “Where does the smell live — the peel or the inside?” “How many segments did we count?”',
 childSay:'Your child might roll every orange in the shop “to wake it up”, announce the smell before anyone asks, or correct you when you call the carrot an orange — “no, that is a carrot, it is ORANGE” — one word, two meanings, both in service.',
 real:'Oranges repeat all winter: the fruit bowl, the juice at breakfast, the smell on a grown-up’s hands after peeling. Every appearance rehearses both meanings — the fruit and the color — and the smell always gets top billing.',
 play:'The sniff station: roll two oranges on the table, sniff your palms, and vote on which smells sunnier. Then a grown-up scores the peel, your child pulls the strips off, and the segments get counted into two bowls before the loud, juicy munching begins. Smell, count, eat — the whole lesson in one snack.',
 safety:'A grown-up starts the peel with a small knife-score so small fingers have an edge to grip; the fruit itself gets rinsed before peeling, because hands travel from peel to mouth. Segments for the smallest eaters get their stringy coats removed first.',
 check:'Ask: “What two things is an orange? Where does the smell come from? Count the boats.” The double word, the woken perfume and a counted segment — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child will not smell the peel, do not insist — sniff your own hands theatrically and wait. Curiosity about why your face is doing that usually wins inside a minute, and self-discovered smells stick harder than instructed ones.'},
'strawberry':{
 title:'Strawberry',one:'The berry that wears its seeds on the outside — bright red, crowned in green, and the fastest recognition win in any supermarket.',
 seoTitle:'Strawberry Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Berry Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach strawberry with where it grows, an eyes-only seed hunt and a wash-and-hull tasting for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The strawberry is the fruit with its seeds on the outside — a fact children find hilarious and remember for years. Here the word is learned at the card, the growing story runs along the ground — low plants, sprawled in the dirt, where slugs also shop — and the seed count is done with eyes only before anyone washes or eats.',
 what:'A strawberry is a small, heart-shaped, bright red berry that grows on low leafy plants near the ground. The card shows a bright red strawberry with seeds and a green crown. The seeds sit ON the outside — hundreds of them — which is why the skin feels bumpy and looks speckled.',
 mean:'Outside-in seeds make the strawberry a botanical celebrity: it is the exception children meet first. A child who knows that strawberries wear their seeds out loud and apples hide theirs inside has started collecting exceptions — and exception-hunting is real science, started at snack level.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the low-growing story — strawberries do not climb, they sprawl near the dirt — and one eyes-only seed hunt before tasting: how many seeds can you count? Too many! That is the answer, and it is correct every time. Then a grown-up washes and hulls, and everyone eats.',
 why:'Looking closely before eating trains observation on a willing subject: the seeds are countable, visible, funny. Fine visual work disguised as a giggle builds the careful-looking habit that later finds differences in letters, numbers and faces.',
 dev:'Close observation with a target (seeds), exception knowledge in botany, fine motor in hulling, counting practice that happily fails (too many!), and patient waiting while the grown-up does the cutting.',
 vocab:'strawberry, red, seeds, crown, sweet, summer',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say strawberry in three parts — straw-ber-ry — a long word for a small berry, said three times.\n2) Tell the growing story: strawberries grow on low plants close to the ground, hiding under leaves — shoppers include slugs, which is why we wash.\n3) The seed hunt, eyes only: look at the card, then at a real strawberry — how many seeds can you count? Too many! They live OUTSIDE. Say it like news.\n4) Tasting job: your child washes the berries in the colander while a grown-up pulls the green crowns off and slices them.\n5) Eat together and rank the sweetness — was the reddest one the sweetest? Let the jury decide.',
 say:'“Strawberry! Straw-ber-ry — what a long word for something so small. Look at it: red all over, wearing a little green crown. And here is the secret — the seeds are on the OUTSIDE! Count them with your eyes... too many! That is why it is bumpy. Now wash them, and a grown-up will do the crowns.”',
 notice:'Study the card together: it shows a bright red strawberry with seeds and a green crown, with the word strawberry underneath. Ask your child where the seeds sit — on the skin or hidden inside — and what the green crown looks like to them.',
 ask:'“Where do strawberries grow — up high or down low?” “Which fruit wears its seeds on the outside?” “What is the green top called?” “Why do we wash them before we eat?”',
 childSay:'Your child might announce the outside-seeds fact to anyone who will listen, count seeds at breakfast with total sincerity, or refuse hulled berries in favor of ones “with their crowns still on” — gardeners call that last one correct.',
 real:'Strawberries are seasonal celebrities: the shop punnets in summer, the pick-your-own fields, the plants some families grow in pots. Any real meeting is a rehearsal — and the outside-seeds fact renews itself at every single one.',
 play:'Seed-spotting detectives: one strawberry each, examined with eyes only — how many seeds can you count before giving up? Then the washing job: your child rinses them in the colander while a grown-up pulls the crowns and slices, and the berries get ranked by redness before the first bite.',
 safety:'Strawberries get rinsed before anything else — they grow close to the ground, and the crowns hold grit. A grown-up pulls the crowns and slices what needs slicing; whole berries are for confident chewers, and the smallest mouths get theirs quartered.',
 check:'Ask: “Where do strawberries grow? Where do they keep their seeds? Who takes the crowns off?” Low plants, outside-seeds and “the grown-up” — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child meets a white-streaked or pale berry, teach the honest version: strawberries wear different amounts of red depending on the sun they caught. Same fruit, same seeds, same deliciousness — the color is a weather report.'},
'grapes':{
 title:'Grapes',one:'One word for a whole bunch of little balls — the sharing fruit, and the classic lesson in why we sit down and cut things small.',
 seoTitle:'Grapes Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Sharing Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach grapes with where they grow, a one-for-you sharing game and the safe-size cutting routine for preschoolers.',
 intro:'Grapes are the fruit of many: one word, dozens of little balls, and a built-in sharing game — one for you, one for me. Vines are where this lesson grows from — climbing plants, heavy hanging bunches — and its kitchen half is the routine that makes grapes safe: washed first, cut small by a grown-up, eaten sitting down.',
 what:'A grape is a small, round, smooth fruit that grows in bunches on vines — climbing plants that farms and gardens train along wires and walls. The card shows a bunch of purple grapes. Green grapes and purple grapes are the same fruit in different dresses, and both hang in bunches that beg to be shared.',
 mean:'A bunch is a first collective noun: many small things, one word. A child who says “bunch of grapes” is packaging dozens of objects into one idea — the same packing that later handles families, flocks and alphabet letters. The sharing game adds the social layer: counting out one-for-you is turn-taking with snacks.',
 know:'Three things to gather before the first grape: the word itself, said at the card; the climbing story, told with a hand that rises — vines go up and up, hanging their heavy bunches from wires — and one counted sharing round. Then the kitchen rule, said plainly and kindly: at your house, a grown-up cuts grapes small, every time, and everyone eats sitting down.',
 why:'Grapes carry the set’s most important safety conversation, and the card is the kindest place to have it: the fruit is beloved, so the rule attaches to something warm. Cut-small-and-sit-down is a habit built by calm repetition, and this lesson starts it early, without a single scare.',
 dev:'Counting and one-to-one sharing, collective-noun awareness (a bunch), turn-taking, safe-eating habits rehearsed positively, and vine-and-climbing plant knowledge that diversifies the growing stories.',
 vocab:'grapes, purple, bunch, share, round, sweet',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say grapes — then ask how many grapes are on the card: more than you can count quickly? That is why the word is a bunch-word.\n2) Tell the growing story: grapes grow on vines that climb, hanging in heavy bunches — farms build them wire roads to travel.\n3) Washing job: your child rinses the bunch in the colander while you explain today’s kitchen rule — a grown-up cuts them small, every time.\n4) The sharing round: cut grapes in a small bowl, and count out one for you, one for me, taking turns until the bowl wins.\n5) End sitting down, finishing together — the rule and the game wear the same chair.',
 say:'“Grapes! A whole bunch of them — one, two, three... too many to count! They grow on vines that climb and climb. Now the kitchen rule: we wash them, a grown-up cuts them small, and we sit down to share. One for you... one for me... what a polite little fruit!”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a bunch of purple grapes with the word grapes underneath. Ask your child how many grapes they can point to, whether the bunch is bigger at the top or the bottom, and what color the grapes are.',
 ask:'“What do we call many grapes together?” “Where do grapes grow — on vines or on the ground?” “Who cuts them small?” “Where do we sit to eat them?”',
 childSay:'Your child might count out sharing portions for the whole family, dolls included, or police the cutting rule with delight — “wait, cut them small!” — a safety habit repeated by the child is the safest kind there is.',
 real:'Grapes appear weekly: the fruit bowl, the lunchbox, the picnic. Each bunch is another round of the sharing game and another quiet rehearsal of the kitchen rule — repetition dressed as generosity.',
 play:'The sharing bowl: a grown-up cuts the washed grapes small into a little dish, and you take turns picking one each — one for you, one for me — counting the rounds until the bowl runs dry. The counting is real, the turn-taking is the deeper game, and sitting down together is the rule that never moves.',
 safety:'This is the card that carries the rule: whole grapes are a choking risk for small children, so a grown-up cuts them lengthwise into small pieces, every time, and everyone eats sitting down. The habit is calm, quick and permanent — and it belongs to this fruit specifically.',
 check:'Ask: “What is a whole lot of grapes called? Where do they grow? Who cuts them, and where do we eat?” A bunch, vines, the grown-up, and sat down — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If cutting feels like fuss, reframe it as bunch service: the knife comes out so the sharing bowl can be refilled. The rule sticks best when it sounds like hospitality rather than homework, and your child will pass it on to visitors.'},
'watermelon':{
 title:'Watermelon',one:'The longest word in the class on the largest fruit in the bowl — one slice, three colors, and summer in every bite.',
 seoTitle:'Watermelon Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Summer Fruit Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach watermelon with where it grows, a three-color naming game and a cold-wedge summer tasting.',
 intro:'Watermelon is the fruit that arrives like an event: enormous, heavy, and full of three colors that the other cards spent a whole class teaching. This lesson teaches the long word at the card, the growing — on the ground, on sprawling vines — and the tap-and-name game that turns a slice into a color review.',
 what:'A watermelon is a huge fruit that grows on the ground on trailing vines, resting on the soil until picked. The card shows a slice: green rind, red flesh, black seeds. The word is long — wa-ter-mel-on — and worth clapping out, because four parts is the longest rhythm in the fruit class.',
 mean:'One slice, three colors: green, red, black — a watermelon is a color lesson wearing a snack. A child who names all three while tapping the slice is doing multi-property description — size, color, position — and the fruit makes the description feel like a drum solo instead of a test.',
 know:'Your child needs the word clapped at the card, the ground-growing story — this fruit does not climb or hang, it lounges — and one tap-and-name round on a real slice: tap the green, tap the red, tap a seed. Then the tasting: a grown-up cuts, cold wedges are served, and summer officially begins.',
 why:'Multi-word description is the bridge from naming to telling: “the red part”, “the green outside”, “the black seeds” are little sentences about one object. Watermelon is the friendliest place to practise, because the object is delicious and the parts are unmissable.',
 dev:'Multi-property description (color and position), syllable clapping on a four-part word, size vocabulary (enormous, heavy), summer-food culture, and patient waiting — the biggest fruit in the shop also has the longest queue to the table.',
 vocab:'watermelon, red, green, seeds, big, summer',
 teach:'1) Show the card and clap the word out — wa-ter-mel-on, four claps, the longest word in the fruit class, said twice.\n2) Tell the growing story: watermelons grow on the ground on sprawling vines — no climbing, no hanging, just lounging in the sun until big.\n3) The tap-and-name game on the card: tap the green rind and say green, tap the red flesh and say red, tap a seed and say black.\n4) Real-slice round: a grown-up cuts a wedge, and your child taps and names all three colors before the first bite.\n5) Eat cold, and rate the seeds — count how many ride home on the slice. The word got clapped; the colors got named; summer got eaten.',
 say:'“Watermelon! Wa-ter-mel-on — four claps for the biggest fruit we know! It grows on the ground, resting like a sunbather. Look at the slice: green outside, red inside, black seeds — three colors in one snack. Tap them and name them... now the best part: the crunching!”',
 notice:'Study the card together: it shows a watermelon slice with green rind, red flesh and black seeds, with the word watermelon underneath. Ask your child to point at each color in turn, and where the black seeds are sitting.',
 ask:'“How many claps does the word need?” “Where does a watermelon grow — up a tree or on the ground?” “What are the three colors in one slice?” “Which color is the outside?”',
 childSay:'Your child might clap out wa-ter-mel-on for days, tap-tap-tap every fruit in the bowl, or report that the watermelon is “sleeping on the ground” — a fruit described as a sunbather is a fruit whose biography has been understood.',
 real:'Watermelons headline the summer: picnics, barbecues, the shop where whole ones sit like boulders. Each meeting repeats the long word and the three-color game — the loudest vocabulary lesson of the warm months, served cold.',
 play:'The three-color drum: a grown-up cuts a cold wedge, and your child taps it like a drum — tap the green and say green, tap the red and say red, tap a seed and say black — before the first bite. Then the seed count: how many rode home on the slice? Count them off the plate, and eat the winnings.',
 safety:'The cutting is all grown-up work: a whole watermelon defeats adult knives, so no small hands go near the job. Cold wedges for little eaters get served seeded or spooned, sitting down — the biggest fruit obeys the same sit-down rule as the smallest.',
 check:'Ask: “Clap the long word. Where does it grow? Name the three colors in a slice.” Four claps, the ground, and green-red-black — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If the four claps tangle, drop to two — mel-on — and grow the word back one clap at a time over the week. Long words are stairs, not walls, and every child climbs them at their own pace.'},
'pineapple':{
 title:'Pineapple',one:'The fruit that wears a crown — spiky outside, golden inside, and full of secrets: it grows in the ground, not in trees.',
 seoTitle:'Pineapple Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Crown-and-Crust Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach pineapple with where it really grows, a bumpy-and-smooth touch test and a grown-up-cut tasting.',
 intro:'The pineapple is the fruit bowl’s royalty: a golden body under armor, wearing a green crown, hiding a secret — it grows in the ground, not in trees, crown or no crown. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the secret, and the touch test that makes the bumpy-skin comparison unforgettable.',
 what:'A pineapple is a big golden fruit with a tough, spiky, diamond-patterned skin and a crown of stiff green leaves. The card shows a yellow pineapple with its spiky green crown. It grows on a leafy plant close to the ground in warm countries — the crown is pure decoration.',
 mean:'The pineapple’s secret — ground fruit, tree-looking crown — teaches children that appearances write wrong stories: what looks like a tree-fruit is a ground-fruit. A child who enjoys being told a plant secret is learning that the natural world has hidden facts, and that knowing them is a pleasure, not a chore.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the secret — in the ground, on a plant, despite the crown — and the touch test: bumpy, hard pineapple, then smooth, soft banana. Two fruits, two skins, one lesson in describing surfaces. The tasting waits for a grown-up, because nobody peels armor with little hands.',
 why:'Contrast descriptions — bumpy versus smooth, hard versus soft — are early science language, and the fruit bowl supplies the pairs. A child who can say “the pineapple is bumpy and the banana is smooth” has built a comparison sentence, which is grammar and observation holding hands.',
 dev:'Tactile vocabulary (bumpy, spiky, hard, smooth), contrast descriptions in full sentences, botanical myth-busting (ground fruit, not tree fruit), patience while the grown-up does the armor-peeling, and tropical-plant curiosity.',
 vocab:'pineapple, crown, spiky, golden, sweet, bumpy',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say pineapple — then ask what the fruit is wearing on its head. A crown! Say the word again like a royal announcement.\n2) Tell the secret: despite the crown, pineapples grow in the ground on leafy plants in warm countries. Say it like gossip — children keep plant secrets well.\n3) The touch test, hands washed: feel a real pineapple — bumpy, hard, heavy — then a banana — smooth, soft, light. Say both pairs out loud.\n4) The tasting is a grown-up job from end to end: the skin comes off with a big knife, and your child receives golden rounds or chunks, washed hands, sitting down.\n5) Close with the crown: draw or cut a paper crown for the fruit bowl’s new monarch — the word got said, the secret got kept, the throne got decorated.',
 say:'“Pineapple! Look at it — it wears a CROWN! But here is the secret: it grows down in the soil, not up in a tree. Feel the skin — bumpy! Hard! Like armor. And inside, it is golden and sweet. The grown-up opens the armor; we do the tasting and the crowning.”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a yellow pineapple with a spiky green crown and the word pineapple underneath. Ask your child to find the crown, count the spiky leaves they can see, and say what the skin’s pattern reminds them of.',
 ask:'“Does a pineapple grow in trees or in the ground?” “What is the fruit wearing on its head?” “Which is bumpy — the pineapple or the banana?” “Who gets to do the cutting?”',
 childSay:'Your child might retell the ground-secret to anyone who visits, stroke the bumpy skin like a museum piece, or appoint the fruit bowl’s pineapple “the king” — a fruit with a title and a biography is a fruit whose word has moved in permanently.',
 real:'Pineapples appear whole in the shop and pre-cut in tubs, and either way the word rehearses: crown up, crown off, armor and all. The bumpy-versus-smooth game extends to the whole kitchen — oranges, coconuts in books, the family dog.',
 play:'The touch museum: a whole pineapple, crown and all, travels from hand to hand — bumpy! hard! heavy! — next to a smooth banana for contrast, while your child announces each feeling like a tour guide. The grown-up removes the armor later; the tasting is golden chunks, eaten sitting down like royalty.',
 safety:'Pineapple armor is strictly a grown-up job: the skin is tough, the knife is big, and the crown is spikier than it looks. Small eaters receive peeled, cored chunks — and the crown itself stays decorative, not chewable.',
 check:'Ask: “Where does a pineapple grow — the secret? What does it wear? Who peels the armor?” The ground, the crown and “the grown-up” — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child refuses the first chunk — pineapples tingle — say why: the fruit carries a tiny bite of its own, a little acid that tickles. Naming the tingle turns a refusal into a sensation worth investigating, and the second chunk often goes down as research.'},
'mango':{
 title:'Mango',one:'The yummiest word to stretch — mmmmm-mango — golden inside, blushing outside, and hiding one big flat seed like a treasure.',
 seoTitle:'Mango Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Sweet Fruit Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach mango with where it grows, a gentle squeeze-ripeness test and the hunt for the big flat seed.',
 intro:'The mango is the fruit class’s dessert course: golden, soft when ready, and pronounced with the mmmmm already built in. Two rituals make the fruit unforgettable — the gentle cheek-squeeze that judges ripeness, and the treasure hunt for the big flat seed — while the biography goes up: mangoes ripen on trees in hot countries, in real sunshine.',
 what:'A mango is a golden-yellow fruit, blushing orange-red where the sun caught it, that grows on trees in hot countries. The card shows a yellow-orange mango with a green leaf. Inside is soft, sweet flesh — and one big, flat seed in the middle, like a treasure the fruit was guarding.',
 mean:'Ripeness is a first physics of food: firm means wait, soft-gently means ready. A child who squeezes a mango like a cheek and reports “not yet” or “ready!” is reading fruit by feel — the same judging that later picks pears and avocados, started young on the sweetest possible subject.',
 know:'Your child needs the word said slowly and hungrily at the card, the tree story — mangoes grow up in the sunshine in hot countries — and the squeeze ritual: one gentle press, like testing a cheek. The seed hunt lands at tasting: a grown-up washes, cuts around the stone, and your child discovers the treasure in the middle.',
 why:'Words with built-in sound play — mmmmm-mango — get rehearsed for the pleasure of the sound alone, and pleasure-rehearsed words stick. Add the ripeness judging and the seed discovery, and one fruit has supplied sound play, sensory science and a treasure story. Few cards earn their keep this thoroughly.',
 dev:'Sensory judging (firm versus soft), gentle-touch control, tropical tree knowledge, sound-play pronunciation, and the narrative pleasure of a fruit with a hidden treasure that explains why the cutting is a grown-up job.',
 vocab:'mango, golden, soft, sweet, seed, tropical',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say mango the slow way — mmmmm-mango — letting the hunger show in your voice. Repeat until your child joins the slow club.\n2) Tell the growing story: mangoes grow on trees in hot countries, ripening in real sunshine until golden.\n3) The squeeze test: your child presses one finger gently against a real mango, like testing a cheek — firm means wait, softly-giving means ready. Report the verdict.\n4) The tasting is staged: a grown-up washes the fruit and cuts around the middle, and your child hunts the treasure — one big, flat seed, discovered and admired.\n5) Close with the golden round-up: what else at snack time is golden? The pineapple card will agree.',
 say:'“Mmmmm-mango! Say it slowly — it is the sweetest word in the fruit bowl. Mangoes grow on trees in the hot sunshine. Is ours ready? Squeeze it gently, like a cheek... soft! Ready! Now a grown-up will cut it, and we will find the treasure — one big flat seed, hidden in the middle!”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a yellow-orange mango with a green leaf and the word mango underneath. Ask your child which colors they can see on the skin, where the leaf sits, and what shape the fruit makes.',
 ask:'“How do we say the sweetest word — fast or slow?” “Where do mangoes grow?” “How do we test if one is ready?” “What hides in the middle?”',
 childSay:'Your child might pronounce mmmmm-mango at every meal for a week, squeeze-test everything soft in the bowl, or present the discovered seed like a trophy — the treasure story does heavy lifting, and children retell it with the pride of real explorers.',
 real:'Mangos appear seasonally: whole in the shop, cubed in tubs, dried in packets. Every meeting rehearses the slow word and the squeeze test — and any seed found at home is a treasure twice, the second time for free.',
 play:'The ripeness panel: two mangos, one gentle squeeze each — your child presses like testing a cheek and delivers the verdict: not yet, or ready! The chosen one gets cut around the stone by a grown-up, and the big flat seed becomes a trophy to hold and admire before the golden cubes disappear.',
 safety:'The cutting is grown-up work from wash to plate: mangoes have a big, hard, slippery stone in the middle, and the knife work around it takes practice even for adults. Little eaters get soft cubes, washed hands, and the seed handed over after the danger has left the room.',
 check:'Ask: “Say the sweetest word slowly. Where do mangoes grow? What hides in the middle?” The mmmm, the trees and the big flat seed — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If no fresh mango is available, the dried version carries the lesson: same word, same sweetness, tougher chew. The squeeze test can wait for the next fresh one — vocabulary does not keep a calendar.'},
'carrot':{
 title:'Carrot',one:'The pointy orange hider of the vegetable drawer — orange below, leafy above, and the classic first crunch of the vegetable cards.',
 seoTitle:'Carrot Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Root Vegetables (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach carrot with where it grows, a top-and-tail observation and a washed-and-cut crunch for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The carrot opens the vegetable half of the class with its best trick: it grows hiding underground, pointy end down, leafy hair up. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the below-the-soil story, and the top-and-tail observation that makes the vegetable’s shape a small mystery solved.',
 what:'A carrot is a long, pointy, orange root vegetable that grows underground, with feathery green leaves showing above the soil. The card shows an orange carrot with a leafy green top. Rabbits agree it is excellent. The orange inside it is the same sunshine-colored stuff that makes it good for eyes.',
 mean:'Root vegetables introduce the idea that food has a hidden half: what you see above ground is only the address, and the meal waits below. A child who understands that carrots grow down and beans grow up has a first map of the vegetable world — and maps make new vegetables easier to meet.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the underground story — the orange part hides in the dark soil, the leafy top catches sun — and one full crunchy tasting: washed, topped, tailed and cut into sticks by a grown-up, then crunched like a rabbit. The loud crunch is the vegetable class’s signature sound.',
 why:'The carrot’s shape invites description work: pointy at the bottom, leafy at the top, long all over. Describing parts and directions — top, bottom, pointy, leafy — builds the spatial vocabulary that later handles “put the book on the bottom shelf” without a demonstration.',
 dev:'Spatial and part vocabulary (top, bottom, pointy, leafy), underground-versus-above-ground plant knowledge, loud confident chewing, washing routine ownership, and the rabbit-connection that makes vegetables storybook material.',
 vocab:'carrot, orange, underground, root, crunch, top',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say carrot — then ask which end was underground: the orange point or the leafy top? Let your child guess before you tell.\n2) Tell the growing story: the orange part grows down in the dark soil, hiding; the leafy top grows up in the sun. Pull-the-carrot is the classic farm picture for a reason.\n3) Top-and-tail observation: hold a real carrot and find both ends — the pointy bottom that lived underground, the cut top where the leaves were.\n4) Tasting job: your child washes the carrots while a grown-up tops, tails and cuts sticks — then everyone crunches like rabbits, loudly.\n5) Close with the orange round-up: what else is orange? The orange fruit card matches — one word, two foods, both orange on purpose.',
 say:'“Carrot! Long and orange and pointy — and it grows HIDING underground! The green hair sticks up, the orange part points down into the dark. Rabbits pull them up. Now we wash it, a grown-up cuts it into sticks... CRUNCH! Can you crunch like a rabbit? Louder! The rabbits will hear!”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows an orange carrot with a green leafy top and the word carrot underneath. Ask your child which end is wider, what the top looks like, and what color the whole vegetable is.',
 ask:'“Where does the orange part grow — up or down?” “What shows above the soil?” “Who else loves carrots?” “What do we do before we crunch?”',
 childSay:'Your child might pull pretend carrots from the sofa cushions, crunch sticks with theatrical rabbit noises, or inspect the leafy tops at the shop — a vegetable with a direction story gets retold pointing down, every time.',
 real:'Carrots repeat all year: sticks in lunchboxes, rounds in stews, the whole ones with feather tops at the market. Each meeting rehearses the word, the direction story and the crunch — the most reliable vegetable in the drawer.',
 play:'The rabbit crunch contest: washed carrot sticks cut by a grown-up, and everyone crunched like rabbits — loudest and longest crunch wins the crown. Then cooked rounds get a turn: soft and quiet, same word. Loud versus soft, raw versus cooked — the carrot teaches both and the rabbits eat the evidence.',
 safety:'Carrots for small eaters are washed, topped and cut by a grown-up — sticks for confident chewers, thinner batons for the newest ones, and grated for the very smallest. Whole raw carrots are a chewing challenge, so the cutting board belongs to the adults.',
 check:'Ask: “Where does a carrot grow? What shows above the ground? Show me a rabbit crunch.” Down in the dark, the leafy top, and a loud demonstration — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child avoids raw sticks but accepts cooked rounds, serve both and describe the difference: cooked is soft, raw is loud. Both are the same word, and either one keeps the lesson alive at dinner.'},
'potato':{
 title:'Potato',one:'The spotty vegetable that hides underground like the carrot — lumpy, bumpy, and the star of the great raw-versus-cooked transformation.',
 seoTitle:'Potato Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Raw-and-Cooked Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach potato with where it grows, a raw-versus-cooked comparison and a spotty-eyes hunt for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The potato is the vegetable children have eaten most and noticed least: chips, mash, jackets — all potatoes, all disguised. Underground is the address this lesson hands over — the same dark hiding place the carrot chose — and the great transformation is the main event: one dusty, hard, raw potato becomes every soft, steaming dinner food.',
 what:'A potato is a round-ish, brown, spotty vegetable that grows underground on plants whose leaves show above the soil. The card shows a spotty brown one with its word beneath. Raw, it is hard and dusty; cooked, it is soft and steaming — and the spots, given time and soil, could grow into whole new plants.',
 mean:'The raw-to-cooked transformation is kitchen physics at its most visible: same potato, two completely different feelings. A child who holds a raw potato and then eats mash has watched matter change state with their own hands — the beginning of cooking literacy, and of why-humans-cook conversations.',
 know:'Two facts carry this lesson: the potato grows in the dark below the soil, the same hiding habit as the carrot, and it exists in two states — hard and dusty raw, soft and steaming cooked — so both get met, one held and one tasted. Then the delight: the little spots are called eyes, and yes, that is exactly as wonderful as it sounds.',
 why:'Potato connects the card to nearly every dinner your child has ever eaten, which makes it a vocabulary multiplier: mash, chips, jackets and roasties are all this word in costumes. Naming the root food behind the disguises gives a child power over the whole menu.',
 dev:'State-change observation (raw versus cooked), tactile comparison (hard and dusty versus soft and steaming), underground-growing knowledge shared with the carrot, part vocabulary (spots, eyes, skin), and menu-connected word power.',
 vocab:'potato, brown, spots, underground, hard, soft',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say potato with a bounce — po-TAY-to — then ask where it grows and wait: underground, like the carrot, hiding in the dark.\n2) The eyes hunt: find the little spots on the card and on a real potato — those spots are called eyes, and planted in soil each one could become a whole new plant.\n3) The raw hold: pass the raw potato around — heavy, hard, dusty — and let everyone describe it in one word each.\n4) The cooked reveal: serve the softest thing on the menu — mash, a jacket, soft boiled rounds — and compare: same potato, completely different feeling. Say both words: hard, soft.\n5) Close with the count: how many potato dinners can your child name from this week? Mash counts. Chips count. The word has been busy.',
 say:'“Potato! Po-TAY-to! Brown and spotty and hiding underground — just like the carrot. Feel this raw one: hard! Dusty! Now taste dinner: soft, warm mash — same potato! It changed in the cooking. And the little spots? Those are its EYES. A potato with eyes — imagine!”',
 notice:'Study the card together: it shows a brown, spotty potato with the word potato underneath. Ask your child to count the spots they can see, what shape the potato is closest to, and whether the skin looks smooth or rough.',
 ask:'“Where does a potato grow?” “What are the little spots called?” “Which is hard — raw or cooked?” “What potato dinners did we eat this week?”',
 childSay:'Your child might hunt for eyes on every potato in the bag, demand “the hard one” to hold at dinner, or announce that chips are potatoes wearing costumes — a menu decoded is a vocabulary lesson winning.',
 real:'Potatoes dominate the week invisibly: mash on Monday, chips on Friday, the jackets at the weekend. Naming them all as the same word gives your child the biggest harvest in the vegetable drawer — one word, dozens of dinners.',
 play:'The potato double-act: hold the raw one — hard, dusty, heavy — and pass it around with one describing word each, then eat the softest possible mash from the same bag. Hard then soft, raw then cooked: your child just watched a transformation and named both of its endings at one meal.',
 safety:'Raw potatoes are for holding, not chewing — the cooking does the digestible work, so the tasting is always the cooked version. The washing, peeling and boiling are grown-up jobs, and small hands get the describe-and-compare roles instead.',
 check:'Ask: “Where do potatoes grow? What are the spots called? Which is soft — raw or cooked?” Underground, eyes, and the cooked one — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child only recognises chips as potato, line the evidence up gently: mash, a boiled round, a raw one. Same word all the way down the table — and the moment the penny drops, dinner becomes a revision lesson forever.'},
'tomato':{
 title:'Tomato',one:'Round, red and shiny as a bauble, wearing a little green star — the kitchen vegetable that scientists argue is secretly a fruit.',
 seoTitle:'Tomato Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Vine Foods (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach tomato with where it grows, the crown-hunt on every tomato and a washed-and-sliced tasting.',
 intro:'The tomato is the shiniest card in the vegetable half and the owner of the class’s best argument: scientists call it a fruit because it grows from a flower, cooks call it a vegetable because it lives in dinner. This lesson teaches the word, the vine it grows on, and the little green crown every tomato wears.',
 what:'A tomato is a round, red, shiny fruit-vegetable that grows on vines in gardens and greenhouses, wearing a small green star where the stem held it. The card shows a round red tomato with a green stem. In this class it sits with the vegetables, where the kitchen has always kept it.',
 mean:'The tomato’s double identity is a child’s first famous argument, and it teaches the useful idea that names can depend on who is talking: scientists vote fruit, cooks vote vegetable, and both are allowed. A child who enjoys the argument has met the idea that knowledge has committees — and that they can join the debate.',
 know:'For this card, gather three things: the word, the garden picture — tomato plants lean on sticks, climbing and hanging their fruit — and the crown hunt, because every tomato, card or real, wears a little green crown on top. The tasting completes it: washed, crowns off, sliced by a grown-up, and admired shiny side up before the first bite.',
 why:'The shiny skin makes tomatoes the best light-study in the bowl: where does the shine come from? A small mirror conversation at snack time — light bouncing off the smooth skin — is early physics delivered on a food your child will eat anyway.',
 dev:'Observation of light and shine, vine-and-stick plant knowledge, the two-committee idea (fruit or vegetable, depending on the speaker), gentle handling of round rolling things, and the confidence to hold a small opinion in an adult argument.',
 vocab:'tomato, red, round, vine, shiny, crown',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say tomato twice, two ways — tomato, tomahto — and announce that both are correct, which children find scandalous and delightful.\n2) Tell the growing story: tomatoes grow on vines in gardens, hanging like little lanterns, with sticks to lean on when they get heavy.\n3) The crown hunt: find the little green star on the card’s tomato, then on every real tomato in the house — every single one wears one.\n4) The roll test, gently: round things roll. One slow roll across the table, caught before the edge — shiny, round and in a hurry.\n5) The tasting: your child washes the tomatoes while a grown-up takes the crowns off and slices them. Admire the shine first, then eat it.',
 say:'“Tomato! Say it twice — tomato, tomahto — both are right! It grows on a vine in the garden, hanging shiny and red. Look, every tomato wears a little green crown, right on top. Find the crown! Now wash it, a grown-up slices it... look how it SHINES. Round, red, and dressed for dinner.”',
 notice:'Study the card together: it shows a round red tomato with a green stem and the word tomato underneath. Ask your child where the green part sits, what the shine on the skin could be, and what shape the tomato is.',
 ask:'“What does every tomato wear on top?” “Where do tomatoes grow — on vines or underground?” “Is it a fruit or a vegetable — what does the kitchen say?” “What happens if we roll it?”',
 childSay:'Your child might hunt crowns on every tomato for a week, referee the fruit-versus-vegetable argument at dinner, or roll one around the plate announcing — “a tomato!” — with a straight face. The argument is the lesson’s encore, and children love being its judge.',
 real:'Tomatoes live in everything: salads, sauces, the pasta tonight, the ketchup tomorrow. Naming the round red original behind the sauces gives your child the source code of half the dinner table — one word, many disguises, all delicious.',
 play:'The crown hunt: three tomatoes on the table, and your child inspects each one for its little green crown, then rolls them — very gently — across the table to prove that round things roll. A grown-up crowns and slices for tasting, and the shiny red rounds get admired before the first juicy bite.',
 safety:'The crowns come off with a grown-up’s hands and the slicing too — small round things and knives are an adult meeting. Cherry tomatoes for the smallest eaters get quartered lengthwise, and the shine is for admiring before the chewing begins.',
 check:'Ask: “What does a tomato wear on top? Where does it grow? What do scientists and cooks disagree about?” The crown, the vine, and the famous argument — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child picks tomato pieces out of everything, keep the word alive anyway: name it in sauces, soups and ketchup. Recognition does not require eating — and the sauce-tomato connection is a genuine decoding skill the kitchen hands out free.'},
'cucumber':{
 title:'Cucumber',one:'The chilliest customer in the bowl — long, green and mostly water, with bumpy skin that gives away its famous cousin.',
 seoTitle:'Cucumber Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Cool Crunchy Foods (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach cucumber with where it grows, the cool-slice trick and the watermelon-cousin skin comparison for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The cucumber is the vegetable class’s quiet science kit: cool to the touch, mostly water inside, and wearing the same bumpy green skin as its famous cousin the watermelon. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the trailing-vine growing, and the cool-slice trick that turns snack time into a temperature experiment.',
 what:'A cucumber is a long, green, cylinder-shaped vegetable that grows on trailing vines along the ground or up supports. The card shows a long green cucumber. It is cool to the touch and crunchy inside because it is mostly water — which is exactly why it feels like the fridge’s favorite vegetable.',
 mean:'Cucumber teaches hidden water: a crunchy vegetable that is secretly a drink. A child who learns that cucumbers are mostly water has planted the idea that foods have insides with jobs — water for cooling, sunshine-colors for eyes, in the carrot’s case — and food science starts exactly there.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the vine story — cucumbers trail along the ground like the watermelon, their cousin — and the cool trick: hold a slice against the back of a hand and report the temperature. Then the tasting: washed, cut by a grown-up into coins or sticks, crunched cool.',
 why:'The cousin comparison — cucumber and watermelon, same bumpy green skin — is early classification: families exist in the vegetable drawer. A child who spots the skin similarity has done real botany with their eyes, and the reward is knowing a secret about two foods at once.',
 dev:'Temperature awareness (cool, cold, room-warm), hidden-water science, plant-family classification by skin, crunchy chewing, and the descriptive vocabulary of long, smooth, green and striped.',
 vocab:'cucumber, green, long, cool, crunchy, water',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say cucumber in three beats — cue-cum-ber — a cool word for a cool vegetable, said with a shivery voice.\n2) Tell the growing story: cucumbers grow on vines that trail along the ground — the same plan as their big cousin, the watermelon.\n3) The skin comparison: look at the card, then at the watermelon card — same bumpy green skin, one long and one round. Families look alike.\n4) The cool trick: a grown-up cuts one slice, and your child holds it against the back of their hand — cool! Cucumbers are mostly water; that is the fridge feeling.\n5) Tasting: coins or sticks, crunched cold, and the verdict collected — is the inside as cool as the outside? Science says yes.',
 say:'“Cucumber! Cue-cum-ber — the chilliest vegetable in the bowl! It grows on a vine, trailing along the ground like its cousin the watermelon. Look at the skins — same bumpy green! Now feel this slice on your hand... cool! That is the water inside, having a rest. Crunch, crunch, cold, cold!”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a long green cucumber with the word cucumber underneath. Ask your child whether the cucumber is straight or curved, what the skin’s color does from end to end, and how long it looks next to the other cards.',
 ask:'“Why does a cucumber feel cool?” “Who is the cucumber’s big cousin?” “Where do cucumbers grow — on vines or in trees?” “What does the inside mostly hold — water or sand?”',
 childSay:'Your child might hold cucumber slices to various warm body parts as emergency cooling, report the watermelon kinship to anyone nearby, or crunch coins with a shiver-noise — a temperature fact discovered by hand is a temperature fact kept for life.',
 real:'Cucumbers repeat all summer: salads, sticks in lunchboxes, the raita side at dinner. Every slice is another cool trick and another cousin citation — the most refreshing repetition in the vegetable drawer.',
 play:'The coolness experiment: a grown-up cuts two coins of cucumber, your child lays one on the back of each hand, and the verdict comes in — cool! colder than the room! Then the taste test: crunchy, wet, quiet. Mostly-water science, with a snack for a conclusion that usually gets eaten.',
 safety:'Slices and sticks are a grown-up’s cutting work, and the ends get trimmed off before serving — the stem end can taste bitter, and life is short. Whole cucumbers are for holding and comparing, not for biting like apples at this age.',
 check:'Ask: “Why is a cucumber cool? Who is its cousin? Where does it grow?” The water inside, the watermelon, and the trailing vine — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If your child rejects the crunch, serve the coins peeled — the skin’s bumpy texture is a common dealbreaker, and the cool-water crunch underneath converts more skeptics than argument ever will.'},
'broccoli':{
 title:'Broccoli',one:'A little tree your child can eat — green, lumpy at the top, and secretly a flower: the vegetable children most love to pretend with.',
 seoTitle:'Broccoli Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Tree-Shaped Foods (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach broccoli with where it grows, the upside-down-tree game and a washed-and-cut little-tree tasting.',
 intro:'Broccoli is the vegetable class’s imagination magnet: hold it upside down and it is a tree, point it at the sky and it is a forest, eat a branch and it is dinner. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the real secret — those lumpy bits are hundreds of flower buds — and the tree game that makes the tasting an adventure.',
 what:'Broccoli is a green vegetable with a thick stalk and a lumpy, bushy top made of hundreds of tiny buds — it grows above the ground in cool gardens, standing up like a small shrub. The card shows a green broccoli that looks like a little tree. It is a flower your child is allowed to eat.',
 mean:'The tree pretence is not a detour from learning — it is the learning: a child who plays at being a giant eating a forest is doing symbolic play, the engine of language. The word broccoli rides on every branch, every chomp and every roar, and pretend-loaded words get more rehearsals than plain ones.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the flower secret — the lumpy top is hundreds of tiny buds, which is why it feels like a forest floor — and the upside-down game: hold it by the stalk, trunk to the sky, and take bites off the branches. Then the tasting: washed by your child, cut by a grown-up, trees for dinner.',
 why:'Pretend play is the cheapest motivator in the parenting toolkit, and broccoli supplies the prop for free: tree, forest, tiny clouds, a green microphone. A child who eats a tree for dinner is eating a vegetable they refused last week — the costume changed, and so did the vote.',
 dev:'Symbolic and pretend play, botanical secret knowledge (flower buds), part vocabulary (stalk, branches, buds), confident chewing of a challenging texture, and the storytelling that turns dinner into a nature documentary.',
 vocab:'broccoli, green, tree, flower, buds, crunch',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say broccoli — then ask what it looks like. A tree! Agree enthusiastically; the resemblance is the lesson’s engine.\n2) Tell the secret: the lumpy top is made of hundreds of tiny flower buds — broccoli is a flower your child is allowed to eat, and the bumpy bits are the proof.\n3) The tree game: hold a real floret upside down by the stalk — trunk up, branches down — and be a giant taking bites off the forest. Roar between bites.\n4) The washing job: your child rinses the florets in the colander, leaf-forest to tap, while a grown-up stands by for the cutting.\n5) Tasting: cut florets, raw crunch first, then the soft steamed version — same tree, two weathers. Which forest does the giant prefer?',
 say:'“Broccoli! A little tree — and you can EAT it! Look at the bumpy top: those are hundreds of tiny flower buds, all packed together. Hold it upside down — trunk at the top! Now be a giant: chomp a branch, chomp a branch... the forest never stood a chance. Trees have never been this delicious.”',
 notice:'Study the card together: it shows a green broccoli with a lumpy top and the word broccoli underneath. Ask your child what the top looks like, whether the card’s broccoli has a trunk at the bottom, and what color the whole vegetable is.',
 ask:'“What does broccoli look like?” “What are the lumpy bits really — leaves, buds or stones?” “Where does it grow — up in the air or under the ground?” “Which is your giant roar — loud or louder?”',
 childSay:'Your child might demand “tree pieces” by name at dinner, stage forests on the plate with mashed-potato ground, or take enormous giant-bites with sound effects — the pretence is the point, and the vegetable is the beneficiary.',
 real:'Broccoli repeats weekly: florets in pasta, trees on the dinner plate, the tiny ones in stir-fries. Each appearance is another chance to play giant — and a vegetable that comes with a game built in gets eaten on a different schedule than one that does not.',
 play:'The broccoli forest: hold it upside down — trunk to the sky — and walk a finger along the branches, naming the florets like trees, then take one bite per branch until the forest shrinks. Washed by your child first, cut by a grown-up after: a tree you are allowed to eat is a tree worth meeting twice.',
 safety:'The stalk skin is tough, so a grown-up peels and cuts the florets into tree-sized pieces — raw for confident crunchers, steamed for the rest. The washing is your child’s proud job; the knife work stays firmly in the grown-up camp.',
 check:'Ask: “What does broccoli look like? What are the bumpy bits? Who is brave enough to be the giant?” A tree, flower buds, and a volunteering mouth — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If the tree game converts a refuser, retire it slowly rather than banning it: from giant-bites to forest-bites to just-broccoli, over weeks. The pretence is scaffolding, and scaffolding comes down a piece at a time when the wall stands.'},
'corn':{
 title:'Corn',one:'A cob of little yellow bricks, wrapped in its own green packaging — every kernel a seed, and every row a line to munch.',
 seoTitle:'Corn Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Kernel-and-Row Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach corn with where it grows, the unwrap-the-present husk game and a munch-a-row tasting for preschoolers.',
 intro:'Corn is the vegetable class’s puzzle box: a cob of golden kernels wearing a green husk like wrapping paper. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the tall-stalk growing story, and the unwrap-and-munch routine — peel the leaves, count the rows, munch across a line like a tiny typewriter.',
 what:'Corn is a tall grass that grows in fields on thick stalks, higher than a child’s head, wearing long green leaves. The card shows a yellow corn cob with green husk leaves. Every yellow kernel is a seed; the kernels sit in straight rows; and the husk is the packaging the plant grew itself.',
 mean:'Corn is pattern made edible: rows, lines, symmetry — a natural grid a child can hold. A child who munches “across a row” is reading a structure left to right, the exact motion reading will ask for later, practised here on a cob with butter. The rows are a gift; use them.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the tall-stalk story — corn grows higher than your head in fields, one cob per stalk — and the unwrap ritual: peel the husk leaves back like a present, then find the rows. The tasting is a grown-up-cooked cob, cooled, and munched across the rows.',
 why:'Unwrapping the husk is delayed gratification with a payoff: the present is food, the paper is a plant. Children who work to open their food value it differently — and the husk-to-kernel reveal teaches, without one word of lecture, that food comes packaged by nobody but nature.',
 dev:'Pattern reading on a real object (rows and lines), unwrap sequencing, munch-across-the-row fine motor, tall-plant field knowledge, and the patience that unwrapping a present — a plant-grown present — quietly builds.',
 vocab:'corn, yellow, kernels, husk, rows, cob',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say corn — then ask how many yellow kernels your child can count before losing track. Too many! That is why they come in rows.\n2) Tell the growing story: corn grows on tall stalks in fields — higher than your head — with long leaves, and the cobs hide inside them.\n3) The unwrap: your child peels the husk leaves back, one by one, like unwrapping a present — the plant wrapped it itself. Announce each leaf.\n4) The row hunt: find the straight lines of kernels — count a row across the top, then run a finger down one full line like a ladder.\n5) The tasting: a grown-up cooks the cob and cools it, and your child munches across one row, left to right, like a tiny typewriter. Ding!',
 say:'“Corn! A cob of little yellow bricks, all in a row. It grows on tall, tall stalks — higher than your head! — and hides in green wrapping paper. Unwrap it: leaf by leaf. Now the rows — count one, then MUNCH across a line, left to right, like a typewriter. Typewriter munching is the only correct way!”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a yellow corn cob with green husk leaves and the word corn underneath. Ask your child whether the husk is open or closed, how many kernels they can point to, and what the green leaves are doing on the card.',
 ask:'“What wraps the corn up?” “Where does corn grow — on bushes or on tall stalks?” “What is one yellow kernel if we plant it?” “Which way do we munch a row?”',
 childSay:'Your child might unwrap any leaf-wrapped thing looking for kernels, munch typewriter rows with sound effects, or report that corn grows “taller than Daddy” — field-height is the fact that sticks, and it is accurate.',
 real:'Corn repeats all summer: cobs at barbecues, sweetcorn at picnics, the popcorn bag at the cinema. Every form is the same word in a different costume, and the row-munching trick upgrades every single cob for the rest of the season.',
 play:'The typewriter munch: a grown-up cooks and cools the cob, your child grips the ends and munches across one row of kernels — ding! — like a tiny typewriter, row by row. Between rows, count the empty lines. Nobody has ever finished this game without a very buttery grin.',
 safety:'The cooking and the cooling are grown-up work — cobs come off the stove hotter than they look, and the knobs of butter wait until the steam is gone. For the smallest eaters, a grown-up cuts the kernels off the cob; typewriter munching is for confident chewers.',
 check:'Ask: “What wraps a corn cob? Where does corn grow? Show me a typewriter munch.” The husk, the tall stalks, and a left-to-right demonstration — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If the typewriter game stalls, invert it: your child drives the row, and you are the carriage that clacks at each kernel. The sillier the sound effects, the further down the row the munching goes — an engineered vegetable victory.'},
'onion':{
 title:'Onion',one:'The vegetable with layers — papery outside, secret inside, and the strongest smell in the whole drawer, from a safe step away.',
 seoTitle:'Onion Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Layer Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach onion with where it grows, a skin-by-skin peeling ritual and the famous eye-watering science for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The onion is the vegetable class’s mystery box: round, brown, papery, and hiding layer under layer under layer. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the underground growing, and the peeling ritual — skin by skin, like unwrapping a secret — plus the eye-watering superpower, watched from a respectful distance.',
 what:'An onion is a round, brown, papery-skinned bulb that grows underground, with green shoots it sends up above the soil. The card shows a round brown onion. Inside, it is rings all the way down: layer under layer, each one thinner, until the middle. Its smell is the strongest in the vegetable drawer.',
 mean:'Layers are a first model of inside-ness: things have insides, and some insides have insides. A child who peels an onion to its middle has physically explored structure — the same idea that later explains earth’s core, tree rings and story chapters. The drawer keeps a geology lesson in papery skin.',
 know:'Gather three things for this card: the word, the dark-soil biography — onions grow below the ground like their root-vegetable cousins — and the peeling ritual, skin by skin, with hands washed afterwards. Save room for the superpower: onions defend themselves with a juice that stings eyes into tears, fascinating to watch — the child sees it from a step away, the grown-up does the cutting, and nobody leans in.',
 why:'The onion offers the class’s only chance to watch a plant defend itself, and children find the crying fascinating rather than sad: a vegetable with a superpower. Naming the cause — the juice, the fumes, the defence — turns a kitchen nuisance into a science moment with a moral: plants protect themselves too.',
 dev:'Layer and structure vocabulary, sequential peeling (fine motor, order), cause-and-effect observation (juice to tears), underground bulb knowledge, and respect-at-a-distance — watching powerful things safely, which is a life skill wearing a vegetable costume.',
 vocab:'onion, brown, layers, peel, smell, round',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say onion from the belly — UN-yun — then ask how many skins your child thinks it wears. Nobody ever guesses enough.\n2) Tell the growing story: onions grow underground like the carrot and the potato, round bulbs hiding in the dark with green shoots above.\n3) The peeling ritual: your child peels the papery skins off, one by one, onto a growing pile — like unwrapping a secret, layer under layer. Count the skins as they come.\n4) The smell test, hands washed after: cup hands over the peeled onion, sniff gently, and rate the smell on the family scale from apple to strongest-thing-ever.\n5) The superpower demo, grown-up operated: you cut the onion at a distance while your child watches from a step away — eyes may water! Explain the defence: the juice is the plant protecting itself. Fascinating, not sad.',
 say:'“Onion! UN-yun — round and brown and wearing a hundred paper coats. It grows underground, hiding like the carrot. Peel it: one skin, two skins, three — layer under layer! Now the magic: when a grown-up cuts it, it makes your eyes water! The onion is defending itself. Plants have superpowers too.”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a round brown onion with the word onion underneath. Ask your child what shape it is, whether the skin looks smooth or papery, and whether the card shows any green shoots.',
 ask:'“Where does an onion grow?” “What is under the papery skin?” “What happens to eyes when it gets cut?” “Why does the plant make eyes water — what is it doing?”',
 childSay:'Your child might peel anything papery in search of layers, warn visitors about the onion’s defences, or report the crying science at dinner — a plant with a superpower gets retold with the drama it deserves, and the drama is accurate.',
 real:'Onions are in nearly every dinner pot: soups, sauces, the frying smell that announces dinner before the table is set. The word repeats from the next room — “onions first!” — and the smell does the advertising all by itself.',
 play:'The layer-by-layer mystery: a grown-up peels the onion while your child counts the skins as they come off — one, two, three — and sniffs the air between layers. The eye-watering finale happens at a safe distance, watched like a magic show: the onion defends itself, and your child gets to be impressed.',
 safety:'All cutting is grown-up work at a distance from small faces — the juice that stings adult eyes stings young ones more. Peeling is your child’s job, hands washed afterwards; the knife, the board and the tears stay firmly in the adult camp.',
 check:'Ask: “Where does an onion grow? What hides under the skin? What superpower does it have?” Underground, layer under layer, and the eye-watering defence — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If the crying puts your child off onions entirely, reframe it: the onion is not being mean, it is being brave — defending itself like a hedgehog. Children forgive plants their defences easily, and the respect makes the tasting, later, feel earned.'},
'pumpkin':{
 title:'Pumpkin',one:'The heavyweight champion of the vegetable cards — round, ribbed, orange, and hiding hundreds of seeds inside its hollow drum.',
 seoTitle:'Pumpkin Lesson for Ages 3–4 — How to Teach Harvest Words (Class 22)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 3–4: teach pumpkin with where it grows, the pat-and-lift heavyweight test and a wash-and-count seed hunt for preschoolers.',
 intro:'The pumpkin closes the vegetable class at maximum volume: the biggest card, the biggest vegetable, and a season — autumn — that seems to exist partly in its honor. This lesson teaches the word at the card, the ground-growing story it shares with the watermelon, and the seed hunt that turns the giant into a counting game.',
 what:'A pumpkin is a huge, round, orange vegetable that grows on the ground on sprawling vines, resting on the soil like the watermelon. The card shows a big orange pumpkin with a sturdy stem. Outside it is ribbed and hard; inside it is hollow, stringy and full of hundreds of seeds in a soft yellow nest.',
 mean:'Scale is the lesson: the pumpkin is the biggest thing in the fruit-and-vegetable world your child handles, and lifting attempts teach weight vocabulary — heavy, heavier, too heavy — with immediate physical feedback. A word that can be tried against your own muscles is a word the body helps remember.',
 know:'Your child needs the word at the card, the ground-growing story — it lounges on a vine like its cousin the watermelon — and the pat-and-lift test: pat the drum like a drum, hard and hollow, then attempt a lift with adult spotting. The seed hunt completes it: a grown-up opens the top, your child scoops, washes and counts the seeds — hundreds, honestly.',
 why:'The pumpkin connects the class to the year’s calendar: autumn, harvest, the season of orange doorsteps. A child who knows pumpkins grow on the ground — not on shelves, not in shop freezers — owns the behind-the-scenes of their own holidays, which is the kind of knowledge children treasure and retell.',
 dev:'Weight vocabulary tried physically, size comparison (bigger than a melon, bigger than a dog?), seed scooping fine motor, vine-growing knowledge shared with the watermelon, and the seasonal calendar knowledge that autumn brings its own vegetable.',
 vocab:'pumpkin, orange, big, seeds, ribs, stem',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say pumpkin in two beats — PUM-kin — a big round word for a big round vegetable, said with arms drawn wide.\n2) Tell the growing story: pumpkins grow on the ground on sprawling vines, like the watermelon — resting on the soil, getting bigger all summer.\n3) The pat-and-lift test: pat a real pumpkin like a drum — hard, hollow, echo-y — then attempt a two-hand lift with a grown-up spotting. Report the weight: heavy? Very heavy?\n4) The seed hunt: a grown-up cuts the top, and your child scoops the stringy nest — then washes the seeds in the colander and counts as far as counting will go. Hundreds!\n5) Close with the season round-up: what else arrives with pumpkins? Leaves, harvest, colder mornings — the year’s biggest vegetable comes with its own season attached.',
 say:'“Pumpkin! PUM-kin — the biggest vegetable we know! It grows on the ground on a long vine, resting like the watermelon. Pat it — can you hear the hollow drum? Now try to lift it... HEAVY! And inside: hundreds of seeds, hiding in a yellow nest. Scoop them, wash them, count them — the biggest vegetable is also a counting game!”',
 notice:'Look at the card together: it shows a big orange pumpkin with a stem and the word pumpkin underneath. Ask your child whether the pumpkin is bigger or smaller than the apple card, what the ribs on its sides look like, and what the stem is doing.',
 ask:'“Where does a pumpkin grow — up high or on the ground?” “Who is its sprawling cousin?” “What hides inside the hollow?” “Is it heavier than your football?”',
 childSay:'Your child might drum on every pumpkin at the shop, report the seed-count in the hundreds with total accuracy, or point out that Halloween pumpkins are “just big vegetables with faces” — a seasonal icon decoded is a vocabulary lesson with decorations.',
 real:'Pumpkins own the autumn: doorsteps, shop displays, the farm visit, the pie. Every sighting repeats the word, and the ground-vine fact keeps coming up — nobody ever guesses it, and everyone remembers it once told.',
 play:'The seed excavation: a grown-up cuts the lid, your child scoops the inside with both hands — slimy! — and the seeds get washed in the colander, patted dry and counted in rows. Roast a handful if you like: the pumpkin pays off its counting game in snacks, and the counting gets louder every autumn.',
 safety:'The opening is strictly a grown-up job — thick skin, big knife, stubborn stem. Your child owns the scooping, the washing and the counting, hands washed after; raw pumpkin flesh is for exploring, and the cooked or roasted versions are the ones that get eaten.',
 check:'Ask: “Where does a pumpkin grow? What hides inside it? Is it heavy or very heavy?” The ground, the hundreds of seeds, and a muscle-tested answer — that is the lesson landed.',
 tips:'If the seed count collapses into silliness — it will — let it: “more than a hundred, less than a million” is a genuine mathematical answer, and the laughing while saying it is the sound of a lesson working.'}
};
